14	// Kubernetes manifests (§3.80). The same discipline as the Dockerfile rules:
15	// an edit that is exactly right or a decline, never an approximation. What is
16	// new is that a manifest is YAML, so a fix may have to add a key rather than
17	// change a token — and that is still a byte-range edit here, computed from
18	// yaml.v3's node positions, never a re-serialisation of the document, which
19	// would drop the author's comments and formatting.
20	//
21	// Three rules, and the ones left out were each left out for a reason:
22	//
23	//   - KSV-0017 (privileged) and KSV-0001 (allowPrivilegeEscalation) name the
24	//     exact field and value to set, and the ids were read from trivy-checks'
25	//     rego sources (checks/kubernetes/privileged.rego and
26	//     can_elevate_its_own_privileges.rego), not guessed.
27	//   - K8S-API-REMOVED is fixed only where the Kubernetes deprecation guide says
28	//     "No notable changes" — renaming the apiVersion is then the whole
29	//     migration. Where it lists changes (Ingress, HPA, PodDisruptionBudget,
30	//     the apps/v1 workloads, CRDs, webhooks…), a rename would produce a
31	//     manifest that validates and behaves differently.
32	//   - runAsNonRoot, readOnlyRootFilesystem and dropping ALL capabilities pass
33	//     their rule and can stop the container from starting: whether the image
34	//     runs as root, writes to its filesystem or needs a capability is not in
35	//     the manifest. It is the USER 1000 lesson of §3.78 — the re-scan would
36	//     approve a pod that no longer runs.
37	//   - Resource requests and limits have no universal value.
38	//   - A seccomp profile can be set at the pod or the container level, and
39	//     which one the author meant is not something the finding says.
